<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The 1994 Vitus 992: a French bonded-aluminium racer sold by former cyclist Henning Jørgensen in Herning, Denmark."/><link rel="canonical" href="https://teob.dk/en/bikes/vitus-992/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href="https://teob.dk/cykler/vitus-992/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://teob.dk/en/bikes/vitus-992/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://teob.dk/cykler/vitus-992/"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:title" content="The 1994 Vitus 992 · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="French Duralinox, Shimano 105 and Campagnolo rims — documented before restoration begins."/><meta property="og:url" content="https://teob.dk/en/bikes/vitus-992/"/><meta property="og:image" content={heroSocial}/><meta property="og:image:alt" content="Purple and silver 1994 Vitus 992 seen from the drive side"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><meta name="twitter:title" content="The 1994 Vitus 992"/><meta name="twitter:description" content="A bonded French aluminium racer sold in Denmark and not yet restored."/><meta name="twitter:image" content={heroSocial}/><title>The 1994 Vitus 992 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/vitus-992/"/>
<main class="atala-page">
<header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">New to the collection · France / Herning · 1994</p><h1>Vitus<br/><em>992</em></h1><p>A French bonded-aluminium frame with a Danish frame number, Shimano 105 and Henning Jørgensen’s name on the tubes. The bicycle has not yet been restored.</p></header>
<figure class="quick-pass vitus-hero"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Purple and silver 1994 Vitus 992 seen from the drive side before restoration" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>The Vitus 992 as it entered the collection, seen from the drive side. Photograph: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1994</strong><span>Dated by frame number</span></div><div><strong>992</strong><span>Vitus Duralinox</span></div><div><strong>105 SC</strong><span>Shimano equipment</span></div><div><strong>WH106K</strong><span>Danish frame number</span></div></section>
<section class="atala-opening vitus-opening"><p class="section-label">Before the work begins</p><div class="vitus-opening-copy"><p>This Vitus 992 is new to the collection. It remains used and untouched, which is a good place to begin. Before dismantling, the frame, transfers, cable routing and wear will be recorded. Polishing can always come later. Putting the original traces back is harder.</p><p>From a distance, the purple and silver bicycle looks unmistakably early 1990s. Up close it reveals how unusually Vitus worked with aluminium: the tubes are not welded together but bonded into large shaped junctions. Those transitions are both structure and visual language.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">1992</p><h2>Vitus made the tubes larger</h2></div><div><p>Since the late 1970s, Vitus had built light aluminium frames by bonding tubes into shaped aluminium junctions. The 979 became familiar throughout the 1980s and accompanied Sean Kelly through a long series of victories in one-day and stage races.</p><p>The principle was simple to describe but demanding to execute. The tubes were fitted into the junctions and secured with epoxy. This avoided the intense heat of welding and allowed Vitus to produce a light frame with a very different character from the traditional steel frames of the period.</p><p>Low weight came with a drawback. The 979 developed a reputation for noticeable flex around the bottom bracket and head tube when a powerful rider pushed hard. Vitus answered in 1992 with the 992. Larger, oval Duralinox tubes replaced the slender round main tubes. Smooth junctions at the head tube, seat tube and bottom bracket gave the frame a more integrated appearance and greater resistance to twisting.</p><p>The teardrop and egg-shaped tube profiles were more than a visual update. Their larger sections allowed Vitus to increase stiffness without abandoning the bonded construction that had become its signature. At the same time, the frame retained the muted, resilient ride many riders associated with the company’s aluminium bicycles.</p><p>Sean Kelly won Milan–San Remo in 1992 on a Vitus 992. The model reached the road just as welded aluminium and carbon began pressing the bonded construction from opposite directions. The 992 therefore occupies a brief technical interval: more substantial and purposeful than the 979, yet still made by a method that would soon become less common in professional racing.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={headTube} alt="Shaped head tube and bonded oval tubes on the Vitus 992"/><figcaption>The oval tubes enter large shaped aluminium junctions at the head tube.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={bottomBracket} alt="Bottom-bracket junction of a purple Vitus 992"/><figcaption>The bottom-bracket area clearly shows the difference from the slimmer Vitus 979.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={seatLeft} alt="Seat cluster and integrated seatpost clamp on a Vitus 992"/><figcaption>A recessed Allen bolt at the rear of the seat cluster secures the seatpost.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">France to Herning</p><h2>Henning Jørgensen’s name on the tubes</h2></div><div><p>The frame is French and the model is a Vitus 992. The Jørgensen transfers tell another part of its history: the bicycle was bought from Henning Jørgensen, a former racing cyclist and bicycle dealer in Herning.</p><p>That distinction matters. The Jørgensen name does not make this a separate model; it records the local route from the French manufacturer to a Danish customer. The dealer’s name on the top tube and down tube became part of the bicycle’s identity, even though Vitus made the frame itself.</p><p>The Danish frame number <strong>WH106K</strong> dates the bicycle to 1994. Together, the number, dealer’s name and surviving build make it more than a loose French frame. They show how it entered Danish cycling culture and became a particular working racer in Herning.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">The working racer</p><h2>Japanese group and Italian rims</h2></div><div><p>The bicycle is built with Shimano 105 SC, the dependable 1055-generation group from the early 1990s. The chainset, front and rear derailleurs and brakes form a coherent working build rather than a display project.</p><p>The shift levers remain on the down tube and can be used in friction or indexed mode. A hand must leave the bars for every change. At the same time, Shimano’s integrated brake and shift levers were already reshaping the racing cockpit. This bicycle sits directly across the transition between traditional controls and the arrangement that would soon become standard.</p><p>Dark-anodised Campagnolo Lambda Strada rims add an Italian element to the French frame and Japanese group. The Lambda rim’s lightly profiled shape and dark surface were familiar on many good road wheels of the period. Here they give the bicycle a more subdued appearance than bright polished rims would have done.</p><p>The combination of a French frame, Japanese group and Italian rims was not a clash of styles. It was a perfectly ordinary way to assemble a good working racer: choose the parts that suited the job and the available money. For that reason, the bicycle says as much about the cycle trade of the 1990s as it does about Vitus construction.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={seatCable} alt="Internal cable routing through the seat cluster of the Vitus 992"/><figcaption>The internal cable runs through the shaped seat cluster.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearBrake} alt="Shimano 105 rear brake and silver seatstays on the Vitus 992"/><figcaption>A Shimano 105 rear brake sits between the silver stays.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Complete Vitus 992 with purple main tubes and silver rear triangle"/><figcaption>Purple anodising, bare aluminium junctions and Jørgensen transfers define the bicycle.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">The collection bicycle</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Maker and model</dt><dd>Vitus 992 Duralinox</dd></div><div><dt>Year</dt><dd>1994, dated by its Danish frame number</dd></div><div><dt>Frame number</dt><dd>WH106K</dd></div><div><dt>Dealer</dt><dd>Henning Jørgensen, Herning</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Oval Duralinox aluminium tubes bonded into aluminium junctions</dd></div><div><dt>Fork</dt><dd>Vitus aluminium</dd></div><div><dt>Gears and brakes</dt><dd>Shimano 105 SC</dd></div><div><dt>Shift levers</dt><dd>Friction/indexed down-tube levers</dd></div><div><dt>Rims</dt><dd>Campagnolo Lambda Strada</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>New to the collection · not restored</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="atala-story period-sources"><div><p class="section-label">Sources</p><h2>Basis</h2></div><div><p><a href="https://vitusbikes.com/blogs/stories/vitus-heritage-a-legacy-of-innovation">Vitus: Vitus Heritage</a> · the bicycle, frame number and information supplied by its owner.</p><p>The construction and components are described from visible markings on the bicycle. The year follows the Danish frame number.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-closing"><p>The 992 kept the adhesive and the junctions but made the tubes larger. This one was sold in Herning, received Japanese equipment and Italian rims, and was ridden. It now stands still briefly while those traces are recorded.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1990-1999/`}>View the 1990–1999 period →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
